--- !u!114 &4907234715283401748
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 685206310170667337}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5c1512ad9b5497649836d85ff0a73b0f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  generalTips:
  - hint: Loading...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Now loading...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Please wait...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Hate these loading tips? Turn them off in the Settings.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Keep your rhythm up!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Every day is a good day for music!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Turn your Try Agains into Superbs!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Asking for advice from other Heaven Studio users is a good way to improve
      your remixing skills.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Knowing how to chart at a slower pace will display your courage and grace.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Support the original Rhythm Heaven franchise any way you can!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: See a remix you like? Open it in the Editor to see how it works!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: '- Removed Corkbrew'
    rerollChance: 0.9
    type: 0
  - hint: "Heaven Studio might be done.\\nBut the new lead developer, Bobby Bobberson?\\nOne
      day, he showed up, uniquely skilled.\\nThey say he gave us a brand new Build.\\nHa
      ha... how can I say so without fear?\\nYou\u2019re reading this loading tip
      right here."
    rerollChance: 0.99
    type: 0
  - hint: There's levels that come with Heaven Studio that are not only fun to play,
      but serve as a great reference in the editor.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  - hint: Recording a level? For a clean look, toggle off the Timing Display in Settings!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 0
  playTips:
  - hint: Keyboard getting old? Try using Touch controls with your mouse!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: The key for Quiz Show is to use the rhythm to remember!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: For Launch Party, each rocket takes three beats... except for the standard
      rocket, which takes four.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: You can change your keybinds in the settings.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Packing Pests starts with <sprite="Buttons" name="SouthOn">!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Nodding along to the beat is a good way to keep your tempo in line with
      the level's. (Alternatively, foot-tapping and finger-tapping work as well!)
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Try practicing the fangames!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Use your ears, not your eyes!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Try playing some level packs!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: With the "Open in Editor" button, you can peek behind the curtains of your
      favorite levels, or even practice hard parts.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Every score you get is added up! You can see your Megaflow in the Library.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: 'Too hard? Try "Tempo Down!" Too easy? Try "Tempo Up!" '
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Make sure to take breaks when playing! The longer you play, the harder
      it is to keep up a rhythm...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  - hint: Shouting in Moai Doo-Wop is simple! A quick snap of the finger, and with
      practice, that's all there is to it.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 1
  editorTips:
  - hint: Use an .ogg file for your music to avoid a big .riq!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Remember to save your level, and save often!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Try new things! Using a genre, artist or game you're less familiar with
      can boost your creativity.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: If your level is in swing, you can right-click on a Tempo Change to set
      the swing.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Some games like Rockers or Glee Club sound much better if you pitch the
      cues to the music.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Need to move lots of blocks in a level? Use the keybinds Ctrl+Shift+I and
      Ctrl+Shift+U to move all events after the playback marker forwards or backwards.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Hold Alt when dragging a block to duplicate it.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Do you know all the secrets of the Editor? Use the Editor Help menu to
      learn the keybinds and other features.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Keeping the beat? Remember to use a count-in so the player knows when to
      start!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Got the perfect moment for a Skill Star? Line up the end of the Skill Star
      block with the input to make sure it works!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Make sure the audio cues are loud and clear! Lower the music volume if
      you need to.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Keep your game transitions in check! Use Toggle Inputs to prevent any accidental
      misses, especially after transitioning out of a keep-the-beat game.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Keep your blocks around the beat lines! If they sound off-sync, make sure
      your BPM and Song Offset are correct.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Every game has its strengths and weaknesses. Not every game fits every
      song, but there's a song for every game!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Markers can be used to split a level into sections, but they can also assign
      different score weights and rating categories to each section!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: VFX can be cool, but try not to overdo it. Less is more!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Often, the key to nice visuals is a consistent color scheme. Use "change
      background" blocks, filters, color grading, and more to give your level a distinct
      style!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Always make sure to check the "Chart Contains Flashing Effects" checkbox
      in the Remix Properties if necessary. Safety first!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Take the time to fill out your Remix Properties. A good epilogue can help
      your level be memorable!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: It's usually advised not to use Mr. Downbeat without a good reason.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Gotten the hang of everything? Want some more control? Switch on Advanced
      Editor Features in Settings!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: If your level's .riq file corrupts, look for an .riq.bak file.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Make sure to always space your Second Contact icons correctly.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Keep it down! If the Cheer Readers' voices overlap, set one voice to "Solo"
      and the other to "Girls".
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Use an Editor Note to remind yourself to add stuff later.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Properly cue Karate Man by putting a yellow or blue lightbulb before every
      Kick or Combo.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Never forget to use an End Remix block once you're all done.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Playtest your own levels! It's the simplest way to check for issues.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Make sure you play levels as well as creating them; inspiration is important!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Experimentation is fun, but learn the rules before you break 'em!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Don't be afraid of asking for help and feedback! The greatest Heaven Studio
      remixes weren't made alone.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: When switching games, line the second, thicker line up with the beat line.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  - hint: Feel like your games are just slightly off with the music? Try messing
      with the song offset!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 2
  sillyTips:
  - hint: Studio Dance\nWhere accreditation is to make Idols Dance,\nTenfold interactions
      with Icons makes one spawn
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: What can you say about a woman with a duck hammer?
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: You can make a nightcore edit of any song by increasing the Playback Speed
      to 1.25x.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: Heaven Studio (Bobby Build)
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: beat bag
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: Tomorrow for sure...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: Cut everything!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: Sumo Brothers & Dog Ninja hide something wild...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 3
  - hint: When the Bobby is Build!
    rerollChance: 0.5
    type: 3
  factTips:
  - hint: Rhythm Tengoku was originally named Rhythm IQ while in development. R-IQ...
      That seems familiar, somehow.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Trick on the Class came from a Rhythm Heaven bootleg game for mobile, and
      used Monkey Watch for its music.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Nail Carpenter originally came from a fangame uploaded to the Japanese
      video site Niconico.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Meat Grinder was originally a mod for Rhythm Heaven Megamix. The player's
      name is Tack, and the boss's name is... Boss.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Leilani is the name of the flytrap in Nip in the Bud, an original game
      made for Heaven Studio Plus.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Karateka Mania was one of the first Rhythm Heaven fangames, allowing users
      to make entirely custom Karate Man levels. Some of its features can still be
      found in Heaven Studio!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: This Bobby Build was originally called the weekly build, and was exclusive
      to archive.org. Dark times indeed...
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Before the days of Decals, people used to paste pixel art mosaics into
      captions to display images.
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: For the longest time, the Editor used to have only 5 tracks. Some may even
      remember it only having 4!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: A .wav file was accidentally copied into the Heaven Studio source code
      more than 3 years ago, and it still remains there today!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Wizard Waltz and Blue Bear are the only Rhythm Heaven levels in a 3/4 time
      signature!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: "Trick on the Class was originally from a mobile bootleg called \u201CRhythm
      Heaven-Fever\u201D - notice the hyphen?"
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Decals were first found in the Heaven Studio Christmas Build before the
      Bobby Build updated to add them!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Back in 2007, SEGA released an arcade version of Rhythm Tengoku. It featured
      upscaled graphics, two-player gameplay, and an extra set of "Tempo Up" minigames!
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: "Tsunku\u2642 is the lead composer and co-creator of the series. He's a
      big deal in Japan, composing for many popular idol groups!"
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
  - hint: Did you know that the host of Quiz Show is actually a demon named Q Maou?
    rerollChance: 0
    type: 4
